# ============================================================================
# TAU-NOMIC TUTORIAL 01 - SPECS AS VALUES
# The one capability everything else is built on
# ============================================================================
#
# This is part 1 of a series that explains tau-nomic - a living, self-amending
# constitution running on Tau - to anyone interested
# Each part is
# a runnable .tau file: read the comments, run the file, check the outputs.
#
#   Part 1 (this file): WHAT ONLY TAU CAN DO - specifications as first-class,
#                       decidable values.
#   Part 2: deciding vs. voting (idempotence)
#   Part 3: the living constitution (a run that amends itself)
#   ...
#
# Run me:  tau -q < nomic_01_specs_as_values.tau
# Verified against: Tau 0.7.0-alpha, build 240788e (alpha syntax may drift).
#
# READING THE OUTPUT: the REPL echoes every line it reads and then numbers
# its results as %1, %2, ... - scan for the "%N: ..." lines; those are the
# answers annotated below. Everything else is echo. On a POSIX shell you
# can filter:   tau -q < file.tau 2>&1 | grep -E '^%[0-9]+:'
# (In the run-based parts 3-6, look for "o0res[t] := ..." lines instead.)
#
# NOTATION IN 60 SECONDS: names like o1, o4, o5 are OUTPUT STREAMS -
# time-indexed variables over a Boolean algebra; o1[t] is stream o1 at
# step t. A formula with a free t inside { } is implicitly closed with
# "always": the law holds at EVERY step. (i-names are input streams;
# time becomes real in part 3 - until then each example is a one-step
# skeleton.)
# ============================================================================

# EXPECTED-RESULTS: 0 T F T F T

set charvar off

# ============================================================================
# 1. THE CLAIM
# ============================================================================
#
# Every serious tool can CHECK a formula:
#   - a SAT solver tells you if a formula is satisfiable,
#   - a model checker tells you if a system violates a property,
#   - a smart contract EXECUTES rules.
#
# But in all of them, the rules themselves are SYNTAX - text, bytecode, AST.
# The system computes WITH them, never ABOUT them. Ask a smart contract
# "is your rule set consistent?" and there is no such operation. Ask Z3
# "is this formula, AS AN OBJECT, compatible with that one?" and you must
# encode it yourself - and the moment rules can rewrite rules, Rice's
# theorem ends the conversation for every Turing-complete system.
#
# Tau's foundation is different: a SPECIFICATION IS A VALUE. It lives in a
# Boolean algebra (the "tau" type). You can take the meet (&) of two specs,
# compare specs with =, quantify over ALL possible specs - and every such
# question is DECIDABLE. Not "usually terminates": decidable, by
# construction, because Tau trades Turing-completeness for decidability.
#
# That single trade is the root of everything tau-nomic does.

# ============================================================================
# 2. A SPEC AS A VALUE - and the meet as "living together"
# ============================================================================
#
# { ... } wraps a specification into a VALUE of the tau Boolean algebra.
# Read { o1[t]=0 } as the LAW "output 1 is never raised" (say: "the system
# never performs the destructive action o1").
#
# The meet (&) of two spec-values is the spec that enforces BOTH.
# The key fact: the meet is 0 (the empty/impossible spec) EXACTLY WHEN the
# two laws cannot coexist. Consistency of law becomes ARITHMETIC.
#
# (Scope note: we use small { } meets here - a handful of values, which
#  is exactly what this tool is for. For LARGE one-off conjunctions,
#  part 7 shows the query form to prefer on current alpha builds.)

# Two laws that contradict each other - their meet is the impossible spec:
normalize ({ o1[t]=0 } & { o1[t]=1 }).
# => %1: 0
#    "never do o1" AND "always do o1" annihilate. No judge, no debate:
#    the algebra itself says these two cannot both be law.

# Two laws that can coexist - their meet is satisfiable (not 0):
sat ({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 }) != 0.
# => %2: T
#    "o1 only with authorization o4" and "o5 only with log o6" live together.
#
#    HONEST SCOPE: "satisfiable together" is a deliberately WEAK bar - two
#    implications can always coexist by simply never being triggered.
#    Consistency is the SKELETON of legality, not the whole verdict. That
#    is by design: the skeleton is what can be decided with certainty;
#    the finer judgments live in HOW rights are written (conditional,
#    scoped obligations) and in the entrenchment machinery of parts 4-6,
#    which give the skeleton its teeth. (Appendix 09 shows those richer
#    right-forms live.)

# The same question with a contradiction inside - decidably NO:
sat ({ o1[t]=0 } & { o1[t]=1 }) != 0.
# => %3: F

# ============================================================================
# 3. ENTAILMENT AS AN EQUATION - law reasoning without a lawyer
# ============================================================================
#
# "Does the rule set R already entail rule r?" is in Tau the equation
#     (R & r) = R
# because adding something already implied changes nothing.
# Here: R = "o1 only if authorized (o4)" together with the fact "o1 happens".
# Does R entail "o4 happens"? It must - that is what the implication says.

normalize ((({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=1 }) & { o4[t]=1 }) = ({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=1 })).
# => %4: T
#    The consequence o4 is already contained. Redundancy, entailment,
#    conflict - all of law's logic collapses into decidable equations.

# ============================================================================
# 4. SELF-REFERENCE WITHOUT PARADOX - the historic wall falls
# ============================================================================
#
# Since Russell, self-reference has been where formal systems break: the set of all
# sets that do not contain themselves; the sentence that says "I am false";
# Goedel; Rice. Every Turing-complete rule system inherits this: it cannot
# decide global questions about its own rules.
#
# Tau quantifies over ITS OWN value space - the space of ALL specifications -
# and stays decidable. Ask: "is there a spec X that equals its own
# complement?" (the Russell-style question):

normalize (ex X:tau (X = X')).
# => %5: F
#    No crash, no paradox, no "undecidable": a clean, provable NO.
#    The question about all specs is itself a decidable question.

# And the universal version - a statement about EVERY possible spec X,
# an INFINITE space of values (here a trivially true one, to show the
# quantifier itself is decidable):

normalize (all X:tau (({ o1[t]=0 } & X) = ({ o1[t]=0 } & X))).
# => %6: T
#    "For all conceivable laws X ..." is a legal, decidable sentence.
#    THIS is what makes guarantees over all future amendments possible
#    (part 5 of this series builds exactly that).

# ============================================================================
# 5. WHY NOTHING ELSE DOES THIS
# ============================================================================
#
#   - SAT/SMT (Z3, cvc5): decide formulas - but formulas are INPUTS, not
#     values. There is no meet of two "problem instances", no quantifier
#     over all instances, no execution.
#   - Model checkers: verify a FIXED system against a FIXED property. The
#     system cannot soundly rewrite itself under a preserved invariant -
#     that question is Rice-undecidable in their world.
#   - Smart contracts / DAOs: rules execute and can even amend rules, but
#     "no reachable amendment chain ever breaks invariant K" is undecidable
#     for them IN GENERAL - they are Turing-complete. (A specific finite
#     contract can be model-checked; but then it is finite and fixed,
#     not an open-ended, self-extending rule space.)
#   - Proof assistants (Coq/Lean): can talk about their own terms, but are
#     not decidable (you write the proofs) and not reactive systems.
#
# Tau gives up Turing-completeness and gains, in one language:
#   specs as values  +  decidable meta-questions  +  execution over time.
# The rest of this series is nothing but this one trade, applied:
# a constitution whose rules are DATA it can decidably reason about -
# while it runs.
#
# NEXT: nomic_02_decide_not_vote.tau - why such a kernel DECIDES instead of
# counting votes, and why that makes Sybil attacks meaningless inside it.
#
# ============================================================================
# END OF TUTORIAL 01
# ============================================================================
